    "math_academy_id": "q-30080",
    "knowledge_point_id": "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem",
    "problem": "Two cars, $A$ and $B,$ are both initially at the point $O,$ and they both depart from $O$ at the same time. Car $A$ travels due east, and its distance from $O$ is given by $3t\\,\\text{ft}.$ Car $B$ travels due north, and its distance from $O$ is $2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{ft}.$ Here, $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the distance between the two cars after $2$ seconds?",
    "worked_solution": "Let's draw a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/aa3f39f65b0785cfca2d1545e4bf331281f523f5e19777372dbb44096bdb4223.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car $A$ and point $O$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car $B$ and point $O$, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between car $A$ and car $B.$\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the distance traveled by the cars after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=3(2)=6\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=y(2)=2(2)^{2}=8\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of the cars is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(t)=4t.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocities are\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(2)=8\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=3\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(3)+2(8)(8) & =2(10)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 164 & =20\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is changing at a rate of  $8.2\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.",
